WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:01.890
Instructor: One other thing about git clone,

00:01.890 --> 00:04.890
something that I do get asked frequently from beginners

00:04.890 --> 00:08.182
is do I have permission to clone this stuff?

00:08.182 --> 00:09.420
The answer is yes.

00:09.420 --> 00:13.140
If you see it on GitHub, if you actually can see it,

00:13.140 --> 00:14.550
you have permission to clone it

00:14.550 --> 00:15.979
and do whatever you want with it

00:15.979 --> 00:18.300
in terms of tweaking the code.

00:18.300 --> 00:19.710
And that doesn't mean you can turn around

00:19.710 --> 00:23.490
and resell it necessarily, depending on how it's licensed,

00:23.490 --> 00:25.650
but you definitely are able to clone

00:25.650 --> 00:27.750
that repository to your machine.

00:27.750 --> 00:31.260
So anybody can clone, if it's a public repository,

00:31.260 --> 00:32.220
and if it was private,

00:32.220 --> 00:33.750
you wouldn't be able to see it anyways.

00:33.750 --> 00:36.750
You wouldn't know the URL, you wouldn't be able to go

00:36.750 --> 00:39.540
to it on GitHub, but if you can see it on GitHub

00:39.540 --> 00:41.010
copy that URL, you can clone it.

00:41.010 --> 00:43.530
You don't need to be an owner or a contributor.

00:43.530 --> 00:45.900
However, you're not allowed to just push up

00:45.900 --> 00:48.210
your own changes, we haven't talked about pushing,

00:48.210 --> 00:50.820
but once we get there, you can't just push changes

00:50.820 --> 00:53.160
up to a repository that somebody else owns.

00:53.160 --> 00:55.920
There's a whole workflow for suggesting changes,

00:55.920 --> 00:57.570
for becoming a contributor,

00:57.570 --> 00:59.910
but you can't just take over someone's repository,

00:59.910 --> 01:01.530
clone it, delete everything,

01:01.530 --> 01:03.210
and then push that up to GitHub.

01:03.210 --> 01:04.723
So that's very, very important to understand.

01:04.723 --> 01:07.980
If you see the repo on GitHub, you can clone it.

01:07.980 --> 01:11.010
Now, everything I've said has been cloning

01:11.010 --> 01:13.260
or everything I've demonstrated has been using git clone

01:13.260 --> 01:17.070
with GitHub repositories because GitHub is so popular,

01:17.070 --> 01:20.010
it's the standard place to host Git repositories.

01:20.010 --> 01:21.210
But remember that git clone

01:21.210 --> 01:23.670
works with any hosted repository.

01:23.670 --> 01:25.360
In fact, if you go to the documentation,

01:25.360 --> 01:27.810
the Git docs for git clone,

01:27.810 --> 01:31.080
there is no mention of GitHub anywhere on here.

01:31.080 --> 01:33.930
So even if that's where a lot of people are using it,

01:33.930 --> 01:35.820
is to clone GitHub repos,

01:35.820 --> 01:37.470
that's just how people are using it,

01:37.470 --> 01:39.510
that is not the nature of the command.

01:39.510 --> 01:42.540
Instead, it works with any hosted repository,

01:42.540 --> 01:44.880
whether that's something that you're serving yourself,

01:44.880 --> 01:47.760
like maybe a company has their own internal server

01:47.760 --> 01:49.020
with a hosted repo,

01:49.020 --> 01:52.500
or maybe we're using something like GitLab.

01:52.500 --> 01:56.280
So I actually struggled to find something that was public,

01:56.280 --> 01:58.470
that we could clone, that was not from GitHub.

01:58.470 --> 02:01.410
But here's an example, this is a project on GitLab,

02:01.410 --> 02:03.060
it's just called Sample Project,

02:03.060 --> 02:04.980
it's really just for demonstrating

02:04.980 --> 02:07.320
how to clone something on GitLab.

02:07.320 --> 02:08.820
So you don't need to go through

02:08.820 --> 02:11.130
and follow along with this, but if you want to,

02:11.130 --> 02:12.120
I'll include the link.

02:12.120 --> 02:13.950
I'm just gonna copy the URL

02:13.950 --> 02:16.680
and then I'm gonna go to my terminal, if I can find it,

02:16.680 --> 02:19.950
over here, I'm gonna make sure I'm not in a repository,

02:19.950 --> 02:24.900
so I am, back out, now I'm not, and I run git clone,

02:24.900 --> 02:27.270
and this time it's not a GitHub URL,

02:27.270 --> 02:31.140
this is entirely different, and it looks like it worked.

02:31.140 --> 02:33.360
I have a new folder called Sample Project.

02:33.360 --> 02:35.340
This is not exciting on the inside,

02:35.340 --> 02:37.170
but you can see I'm in a Git repo,

02:37.170 --> 02:39.030
I'm on the master branch right now.

02:39.030 --> 02:40.050
Git status.

02:40.050 --> 02:41.400
Here's Git log.

02:41.400 --> 02:43.590
There's four or so commits in there.

02:43.590 --> 02:47.160
Alright, so just proof that git clone is not tied to GitHub,

02:47.160 --> 02:49.170
it's just a native git command.

02:49.170 --> 02:52.560
Most of the usage you'll see online is with GitHub,

02:52.560 --> 02:55.113
but it will clone any hosted repo.
